<h1>About</h1>

<p class="lede-inline">
  Flim is a personal project by Panu Hällfors — 
  building a Matrix messenger I actually want to use for voice messaging.
</p>

<h2>Who</h2>
<p>
  Hi — I’m Panu! A CTO and advisorby day in various businesses, working on Flim in my free time. 
  If something feels unfinished, that’s because it probably is — and at the same time,
  I want Flim to be of real use, so all feedback is super welcome — please reach out via email to
  <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>.
</p>

<h2>How it started</h2>
<p>
  Credit where it’s due: this whole thing got rolling when guys called Timo and Jaakko asked
  me to join a voice-message
  business. They put their finger on a real, pressing need — the problem of skimming through
  a pile of voice messages, all looking the same, not knowing which one is the one you're looking for,
  and not knowing what they're about before you even listen to them.
</p>
<p>
  I saw that need too. I just didn’t end up building the business. I ended up
  building an app for my friends instead — and after first creating a closed-source prototype,
  I wanted it open source instead, and working on a battle-tested and secure infrastructure, so 
  I re-implemented Flim as a <a href="https://matrix.org/">Matrix</a> messenger, forking the
  <a href="https://element.io/">Element&nbsp;X</a> codebase that I consider by far the best Matrix client out there.
  The source lives on
  <a href="{{ site.githubUrl }}">GitHub</a>
  (app fork:
  <a href="{{ site.iosGithubUrl }}">flim-element-x-ios</a>).
</p>

<h2>Why</h2>
<p>
  Once that spark landed, two things became the main drivers for me:
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Voice messages are hard to navigate.</strong> They’re lovely when
  you’re sending them — messy when you’re trying to find “the one about Tuesday”
  in a thread of identical waveforms. Without titles (and a bit of summary),
  you’re stuck scrubbing and guessing. Flim leans into making voice messages
  something you can skim, not only something you can play.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>One-hand use was a pain for the everyday stuff.</strong> Opening a chat with a friend
  and closing it again should be as effortless as it is to send a new voice message: with one flick of a finger.
  However, in most
  messenger apps, you're reaching for the top left corner for the back button (which is not where
  your hands are if you're right-handed) or using a
  swipe gesture to close the chat (which very often competes with the swipe gestures on the messages
  itself, and you end up deleting or marking messages instead of navigating back).
</p>

<h2>What Flim is (and isn’t)</h2>
<p>
  Flim runs on <a href="https://matrix.org/">Matrix</a> — a way to deliver messages between people’s phones, without everything having to go through one
  company’s app. Compared with WhatsApp, for example, you’re not locked into a
  single provider: different apps and services can talk to each other on the same
  network. And Flim is one of those apps - the one where your voice messages are titled and summarized.
  And yes, you can send voice messages from Flim to other Matrix clients - the voice message titles and summaries
  will be just shown a bit differently there. Under the hood, Flim is a fork of (a modification of)
  <a href="https://element.io/">Element&nbsp;X</a> — that project already
  did a lot of the hard work for a modern Matrix client. Browse the code on
  <a href="{{ site.githubUrl }}">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
   You do need a <em>homeserver</em>, though — a place that holds your account and
  helps pass messages along, a bit like the mail room for your chats. You can create
  an account on a homeserver of your own choice; if you will, Flim will take you to
  <a href="https://matrix.org/">matrix.org</a>’s homeserver by default.
</p>
<p>
  It isn’t a new chat protocol, a social network, or a promise that every
  Element feature will show up tomorrow with a Flim sticker on it. It’s an app
  for fellow people who prefer voice messages.
</p>

<h2>Transcription and AI summaries</h2>
<p>
  To provide voice titles and summaries, Flim needs some kind of speech-to-text solution (and a bit of
  summarising). The philosophy is simple: <strong>you decide where that work
  happens</strong>, and I do my very best to help you make it as secure and private as possible.
</p>
<p>
  The hope — honestly — is that every phone eventually carries good enough
  on-device AI that nothing sensitive has to leave the device at all. We’re not
  fully there for everyone yet. So for now you can pick: on-device when your
  phone can handle it, a service you choose yourself (i.e. which AI service provider you trust),
  or Flim’s own option.
</p>
<p>
  If you use <a href="/summaries/">Flim Summaries</a>, I’ve tried to make it as
  privacy-friendly as I know how: account email (and plan state) stay while the
  account exists; IP addresses for rate limiting/troubleshooting for about 30
  days, not linked to message content; voice audio, transcripts, titles and
  summaries are not kept after the request. Processing in the EU. Details:
  <a href="/summaries/privacy">Summaries privacy</a>.
</p>

<h2>How to think about it</h2>
<p>
  Use it if it helps. Tell me when it doesn’t
  (<a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>) and I'll do my best to help. My aim is to keep the
  product close to the upstream Element X, only adding Flim's specific features, so if you feel you need a feature, you should primarily raise it with the Element X team.
</p>
